{
  "id": "L-007",
  "title": "Limits at Infinity and Horizontal Asymptotes",
  "atom_ids": ["A-07"],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [
        { "type": "text", "text": "The far ends of the real line" }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "So far the input has approached a finite point. The same question can be asked of the far ends of the real line: what do the outputs do as "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "x" },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " grows without bound? The notation "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "\\lim_{x \\to \\infty} f(x) = L" },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " means the values of "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "f(x)" },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " settle arbitrarily close to "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "L" },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " once "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "x" },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " is large enough. The "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "x \\to \\infty" },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " here is a direction, not a point, so the precise definition in L-009 does not apply to it directly."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "definition",
      "term": "Horizontal asymptote",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The line "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "y = L" },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " is a horizontal asymptote of "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "f" },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " when "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "\\lim_{x \\to \\infty} f(x) = L" },
        { "type": "text", "text": " or " },
        { "type": "math", "source": "\\lim_{x \\to -\\infty} f(x) = L" },
        { "type": "text", "text": "." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "For rational functions the leading terms decide. The standard move is to divide numerator and denominator by the highest power of "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "x" },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " that appears in the denominator, which turns every lower term into a "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "1/x^k" },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " that tends to zero."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "title": "The leading terms decide",
      "problem": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Find "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "\\lim_{x \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{2x + 1}{x - 1}" },
        { "type": "text", "text": " and " },
        { "type": "math", "source": "\\lim_{x \\to -\\infty} \\dfrac{2x + 1}{x - 1}" },
        { "type": "text", "text": "." }
      ],
      "solution": [
        [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Divide numerator and denominator by "
          },
          { "type": "math", "source": "x" },
          { "type": "text", "text": ": " },
          { "type": "math", "source": "\\dfrac{2 + \\frac{1}{x}}{1 - \\frac{1}{x}}" },
          { "type": "text", "text": "." }
        ],
        [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "As "
          },
          { "type": "math", "source": "x \\to \\infty" },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " the terms "
          },
          { "type": "math", "source": "\\frac{1}{x}" },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " tend to zero, so the quotient tends to "
          },
          { "type": "math", "source": "\\dfrac{2 + 0}{1 - 0} = 2" },
          { "type": "text", "text": "." }
        ],
        [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The same computation applies as "
          },
          { "type": "math", "source": "x \\to -\\infty" },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": ", since "
          },
          { "type": "math", "source": "\\frac{1}{x} \\to 0" },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " there as well. Both limits are 2, so "
          },
          { "type": "math", "source": "y = 2" },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " is a horizontal asymptote on both sides."
          }
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "src": "figures/horizontal-asymptote.png",
      "atom_id": "A-07",
      "caption": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The graph of "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "y = \\dfrac{2x + 1}{x - 1}" },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " flattens toward the dashed line "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "y = 2" },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " as "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "|x|" },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " grows in either direction. At "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "x = 100" },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " the value is about 2.03 — close to the asymptote, still not on it."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "widget",
      "name": "plot",
      "version": "1.0",
      "parameters": {
        "series": [
          {
            "label": "y = (2x + 1)/(x - 1)",
            "points": [
              [1.2, 17.0], [1.4, 9.5], [1.6, 7.0], [1.8, 5.75],
              [2.0, 5.0], [2.5, 4.0], [3.0, 3.5], [4.0, 3.0],
              [5.0, 2.75], [6.0, 2.6], [8.0, 2.43], [10.0, 2.33],
              [14.0, 2.23], [20.0, 2.16]
            ]
          }
        ],
        "xlabel": "x",
        "ylabel": "y",
        "title": "y = (2x + 1)/(x - 1) settles toward y = 2"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [
        { "type": "text", "text": "Three cases, one pattern" }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": true,
      "items": [
        [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Equal degrees: the limit is the ratio of the leading coefficients — "
          },
          { "type": "math", "source": "2/1" },
          { "type": "text", "text": " in the example above." }
        ],
        [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Numerator degree larger: the quotient does not settle — it grows without bound, and the limit does not exist (L-008 gives the precise language)."
          }
        ],
        [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Denominator degree larger: the quotient tends to 0."
          }
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "kind": "note",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A horizontal asymptote describes end behaviour, nothing else. A curve may cross the line "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "y = L" },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " finitely many times and still have it as an asymptote: the approach governs large "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "|x|" },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": ", not every "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "x" },
        { "type": "text", "text": "." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "formative",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Explain, in one or two sentences, why "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "\\lim_{x \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{x^2 + 1}{x}" },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " does not exist, and contrast it with the limit in the worked example."
        }
      ],
      "kind": "free_response"
    }
  ]
}